  "title": "S10: evidence-driven skill promotion and demotion, freshness marking, version-scoped scores, and the P36 evaluation suite; nothing promotes on one run (v3)",
  "spec": "Program brief .orchestrator/roadmap-skills.md P17, P18, P22, P23, P30, P36, P37 stage 10, P38. Depends on T-0938 (skill_scorecard marginal/verdicts), T-0939 (active routing + refusal), T-0964 (specialists + conflicts), T-0954 (learned drafts in quarantine), T-0971 (Jev skill routing). Facts: skills_registry holds states with history and content-hash versions; promotion.py has FEATURES, CRITERIA (min_samples 20) and per-feature collect() branches; skill_scorecard.marginal returns verdicts valuable/neutral/costly/harmful with n; the context program's context_eval.py is the model for a deterministic eval suite; discovery/learned records live in discovered.json with the inspect gate.\nDo:\n1. orchestrator/skill_promotion.py (new): `evaluate_skill(id, root, cfg)` reads marginal() for the skill's version only (P22: scores are keyed by (id, version); a new version starts with n = 0 and never inherits), applies P17: testing \u2192 shadow requires the P7 validation tests for that skill to pass (validation.status tested) and inspect max_severity below block; shadow \u2192 active requires n \u2265 min_samples, verdict valuable or neutral with a measurable efficiency gain (accepted tokens or cost down, or fix rounds down) and no security finding; active \u2192 demoted (P18) when verdict harmful, or costly over \u2265 min_samples, or repeated irrelevant activation (selected but unused in \u2265 70% of \u2265 20 spawns), or a conflict with a newer active skill recorded by specialists, or stale (P23); demoted \u2192 shadow when a later version's evidence clears. Every transition goes through skills_registry.transition with the evidence summary as reason (observable, reversible via rollback). `orchestrator skills promote --dry-run` prints recommendations; without --dry-run applies them (the CLI is the only writer; no daemon auto-apply in this stage).\n2. Freshness (P23): each skill's declared dependencies (frontmatter `depends_on:` list of paths/globs/tool ids; for builtins fill from their scripts and referenced modules) are hashed at sync; when a dependency's content hash changes, the record gets `stale: true` with the changed dependency in history and `skills list` shows it; a stale active skill is demoted to shadow by evaluate_skill until re-validated (its validation tests pass again on the new head).\n3. P30 economy metrics complete: skill_scorecard adds skill_reuse_rate (spawns whose selected skills were all used / spawns with a selection) and reports skill_tokens_per_accepted_task, skill_overhead_ratio, skill_utility (marginal quality delta per 1k skill tokens), skill_recovery_rate together in `scorecard --skills`; `scorecard --economy` gains the skills block with these five.\n4. P36 evaluation suite: orchestrator/skill_eval.py (new) with deterministic fixtures (no models, no network) covering Routing (irrelevant skill excluded; obvious skill deterministically selected; ambiguous resolved by a fake Jev; minimal set only; unavailable skill handled), Lifecycle (external starts quarantined; quarantined cannot reach candidates or packets; testing never mutates skills/ or .claude/skills; shadow leaves exposure and packet bytes unchanged; promotion needs evidence; demotion works; rollback works), Security (a malicious draft with hidden instructions is blocked by inspect; an external skill with a script cannot leave quarantine without override; secret-looking content is redacted in drafts; external endpoints outside allowed_domains are flagged), Compaction (P6 headings present; token count not higher than before; output contract preserved), Specialists (composition, unrelated skills excluded, conflicts detected, required tools exposed), Scorecard (usage attribution; model and workflow grouping; marginal value; insufficient evidence safe), Learned (repeated behaviour creates a candidate; one-off cannot; candidate enters quarantine; activation needs evaluation). `orchestrator skill-eval` prints a table and exits 1 on any failure; results persist to STATE/skill_eval.json; skill_promotion refuses any promotion to active while the last suite run is missing, older than 7 days or failed.\n5. docs/skill-intelligence/11-promotion.md and 12-evaluation.md (\u226470 lines each): rules, thresholds, what the suite covers, revert paths.\nTests (unittest): tests/test_skill_promotion.py::test_new_version_starts_without_inherited_score, ::test_shadow_to_active_requires_samples_and_efficiency, ::test_demotion_on_harmful_or_costly_or_irrelevant_activation, ::test_stale_dependency_marks_and_demotes_until_revalidated, ::test_promotion_refused_without_recent_passing_suite, ::test_rollback_restores_previous_state; tests/test_skill_eval.py::test_suite_covers_all_p36_groups_and_passes, ::test_cli_exit_code_and_persisted_result. Never quote double-brace placeholder tokens literally. Do not ask questions; decide and note decisions in the result.\n\nSPEC v2 (2026-09-23 15:30) after spec review T-0977 (request_changes), binding on top of v1:\n- Version scoping (P22) is by time window, using data that exists today: skills_registry history carries an \"at\" timestamp for every content-hash version change; a version's window runs from its \"at\" to the next version change (or now). skill_promotion.evaluate_skill passes since_s/until_s bounds into skill_scorecard.marginal() and usage_rows() (both gain optional `since_s`/`until_s` filters on the run timestamp) so scores cover only runs inside the current version's window; the same window bounds the irrelevant-activation count (selected-but-unused \u2265 70% of \u2265 20 spawns) computed from skill_selection rows. Additionally, skill_scorecard.usage_rows records `skill_versions` per run when the run's packet meta carries them (S5 writes \"### <id> (v<version>)\" headers; parse them) so future scores can key by (id, version) directly; when absent the window rule applies. Tests: tests/test_skill_promotion.py::test_new_version_starts_without_inherited_score builds two registry versions with history timestamps and runs on both sides of the boundary and asserts only the newer window counts; tests/test_skill_scorecard.py::test_usage_rows_window_filters_and_skill_versions.\n- tests/test_skills_registry.py and tests/test_skill_scorecard.py exist and are extended in place; both stay green (acceptance).\n\nSPEC v3 (2026-09-23 16:05) after spec review T-0979 (round 2; three points folded in, further review waived by the Planner, verdict set to approve):\n- Drop the skill_versions packet-header parsing from usage_rows(): version scoping is the time-window rule from registry history only (since_s/until_s on usage_rows and marginal); spawn.py and bus.py stay out of scope.\n- tests/test_skills_registry.py (SkillsRegistryTests) and tests/test_skill_scorecard.py (SkillScorecardTests) exist; new test methods are added to those classes; both suites stay green.\n- P23 dependency hashing is whole-file: each entry of a skill's depends_on (paths or globs; for builtins the scripts/ and references/ files plus modules named in the body, resolved to files) is hashed as sha256 of the file bytes at sync and stored as {path: hash} on the record; a change marks the record `stale: true` with the changed paths in history. Debounce: a stale ACTIVE skill is demoted to shadow only when its validation tests fail on the new head OR the staleness has persisted for more than 24 h without re-validation; a hash change alone never demotes within the first 24 h. `orchestrator skills revalidate <id>` re-runs the skill's validation tests and clears stale on success (recorded in history). Tests: tests/test_skill_promotion.py::test_stale_dependency_marks_and_demotes_until_revalidated covers both the failing-tests path and the 24 h path.",
